00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:03.000
You know, if there's one thing to say about my favorite Pixar movie Wall-E,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.700
it's that it's not exactly subtle with its theme.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.640
The earth has been transformed into a post-apocalyptic wasteland;

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:12.300
left in a ruined state by humanity's wasteful consumerism.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.420
And not in a cool way like Sakaar in Thor: Ragnarok.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.180
Plastic sporks, Rubik's cubes,

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:23.680
leftover Twinkies, the kinds of trash that you and I produce on a daily basis litter every square inch of the planet.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.440
The message is beaten over our head:

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:30.420
"We didn't take care of our earth, and now you've inherited our problems. Other guy: 'We didn't listen!'

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:31.440
"We didn't listen!"

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.240
Every soup tin that you toss,

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.400
every banana peel you give the slip,

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.560
and every Diet Coke can that gets canned is part of the problem.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:43.200
You, you dear viewers sitting in the theater, eating your jumbo tub of popcorn

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.900
and your mega sized Coke, have made this earth uninhabitable

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:47.520
with your wanton lust for more.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.640
More stuff, more things,

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.700
and now, humanity has been forced to take to the stars while these

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:57.120
innocent little robots :3 have been forced to clean up our mess for the last 700 years.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.500
[BnL Starliner Ad] BnL Starliner, leaving each day. We'll clean up the mess while you're away!

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.300
This movie wants to give us a wake-up call.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:07.620
To get us to go green and keep blue.™ To crush us with guilt every time

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:13.040
we get an Amazon order and see all those little air pouches that come packaged with it that you just gotta pop,

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:13.700
b-- before you can

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:17.480
throw them all away, 'cause they take up so much damn room in the garbage!

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.420
And while I hope all the Theorists out there are for environmental responsibility,

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.140
there's one thing that I've learned from years of watching movies and TV shows,

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:28.960
and that's: You can't believe everything you're told at face value. (The Theorist's Code)

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.200
And Wall-E here, for as much as I love it,

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.280
is no exception. :(

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:36.680
When you actually stop and question the events of the movie, you start to realize that

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.400
something sinister is afoot. *muah-ha-ha*

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:43.960
A planet covered in garbage is just one part of a larger conspiracy in the Pixar universe,

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.700
and today I'm gonna prove to you that everything

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:52.020
we've been led to believe about Wall-E and its trash-filled cities is complete and total

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:52.920
GARBAGE.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:59.720
In the opening scenes of Wall-E, we see abandoned buildings, light poles, street signs and other indicators that this was once a thriving metropolis.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:05.000
This indicates the breakdown of one of the most basic things required for a modern city to function:

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.480
a waste management system that transports waste out of the city towards landfills.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.019
I don't know if you've ever stopped to think about it,

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:15.000
but nearly all of a city's trash is transported out of the city to landfills that could be hundreds

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.040
to landfills that could be hundreds or even thousands of miles away from city limits.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:24.220
And though that might seem like a dirty business, both literally and figuratively, waste disposal

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:25.790
is big money.

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:30.300
Contractors get into bidding wars over the ability to take away a city's trash.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.900
"And then, I start eating garbage. I'm the trash man."

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:38.460
Because not only does it allow you to collect on a city's tax dollars - you know, all that stuff

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:40.820
that's taken out your paycheck for sanitation expenses -

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:44.140
but landfill waste actually tends to produce methane gas,

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:48.180
which many landfills are designed to capture and turn back into electricity.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:52.820
Just two landfill gas to energy facilities can generate enough electricity to power

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:55.220
16,000 homes.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:55.730
So

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:57.700
basically, trash collectors are double dipping:

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:03.160
payment to take out the garbage, and then extra payment for selling electricity made from that garbage

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:04.160
back to the city.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:05.580
It's a fantastic business.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:09.780
If, in Wall-E, humanity can afford to create rockets that transport everyone off the planet,

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:15.000
then it can absolutely, very easily afford to have trucks deliver trash to landfills.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:18.579
As long as there is space literally anywhere in the world,

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:21.129
we're not gonna have the problem that's depicted in Wall-E

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:26.589
Which means that, for the situation to have gotten this bad, the world must have run out of space for landfills.

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:29.079
Except, that one isn't gonna be true either.

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:33.849
You see, trash is a problem that people in science and business have been studying for a long time.

00:04:33.849 --> 00:04:38.280
One of those people was the self-proclaimed garbologist Dr William Rathje.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:42.720
An archaeologist professor at University of Arizona who turned his powers of archaeology

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:46.060
not toward studying dinosaur bones or millennia old human remains,

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:49.340
but studying the way that humans manage and treat trash.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.540
Hey, not all archaeologists are gonna be Indiana Jones, okay?

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:54.500
[Indiana Jones] That belongs in a museum!

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:57.900
According to an article by Dr. Rathje in Smithsonian Magazine,

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:02.080
the total amount of land mass needed to contain all of our trash isn't that large.

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:06.200
A single landfill 120 feet deep and 44 miles square

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:10.420
would be able to handle the US' waste for the next hundred years.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:14.220
Now, granted, that's a landfill the size of Delaware, which sounds like a lot,

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:16.460
but remember that's our second smallest state.

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:20.960
It's one twentieth of one percent of the US' total landmass.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.460
Point-zero-zero-zero-five.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:29.480
And that's to handle all of the waste created by the entire US for a hundred years.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:33.720
If we kept filling landfills at the same rate we do now, after ten thousand years

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:38.460
we would still have only covered five percent of our total available area.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:40.980
TEN THOUSAND YEARS LATER!

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:46.420
In short, the prospect of running out of space for all our trash any time near the future just isn't realistic

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:49.700
and certainly not realistic in the seven hundred year future of Wall-E.

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:52.580
Not even in the ten thousand year hypothetical future.

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:54.700
Besides, by the time humanity gets that far,

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.140
we'll probably have other ways of dealing with trash. More efficient ways.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:05:58.800
Or, you know,

00:05:58.800 --> 00:05:59.110
just

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:01.440
launching it into space and sending it out into the Sun,

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:02.800
but I know what you're thinking.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.880
All of that is at our current rate, right?

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:07.340
What about the future? We're gonna get more and more wasteful

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:10.160
and generate more and more garbage as the years go on, right?

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:11.400
That's what I was thinking too,

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:14.300
but when you actually sit down and do the research, this

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:16.360
assumption turns out to be incorrect.

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:20.380
For as weird as it might seem, as time goes on and our society becomes more advanced,

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:23.900
the amount of waste we produce goes downward.

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:28.100
Every year, we're figuring out ways to make our packaging more and more efficient.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:32.260
In the 1950s, we replaced heavy and dense glass bottles with plastic bottles.

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:35.520
Then we replaced those plastic bottles with thinner bottles.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.940
You see it all the time on items like water bottles:

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:41.020
this bottle is designed to use 20% less plastic.

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:45.620
And this waste reduction is all thanks to companies being run under the idea that

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:47.100
green is good.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:48.720
But, uh, not environmental green.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:49.800
huhaaehauah nohohohoho

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:51.880
It's not nearly that altruistic of a goal.

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:54.000
MONEY! Money green is good.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.500
When a company figures out how to make a bottle with 20% less plastic,

00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:59.360
sure, it's good for the environment,

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:00.960
but it's also good for the bottom line.

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.200
It means they saved money by manufacturing 20% less plastic.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:07.540
Packaging foam gets slimmer every year. Why?

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:09.540
Not because companies care about the environment,

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:13.040
but because the less packaging material you use, the less it costs,

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:16.620
and the more items that you can fit into a shipping container or a warehouse.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:21.720
This is one of the places where businesses driven by profit actually make the world more sustainable,

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:24.520
even when they're not intentionally trying to be environmentally friendly.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.400
"Being green" simply nets you more green.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:31.680
Our intuition would say that more development equals more consumption equals more waste,

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:36.240
but in the world right now, less advanced societies tend to produce more waste

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:40.900
because they don't benefit from all the innovations that we have in producing more efficient packaging;

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:43.480
food preservation techniques like refrigeration;

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:48.360
and a transportation network that ensures that food is always getting to the people who are able to use it,

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:50.860
rather than being unsold and left to rot.

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:53.240
The average person living in New York City right now

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:57.000
generates less waste than a person living in Mexico City.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.020
In short, as the world gets more and more technologically developed,

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:03.540
we're gonna produce less and less waste, not more.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:04.820
Even things like saving paper!

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:06.480
We do things all digitally now,

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.180
so we've saved tons and tons of paper!

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:08.820
All of this:

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:13.180
the obvious profit motives that companies have to deal with our city's trash problem;

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:17.460
the indirect profit motive of companies wanting to use less materials and make things more efficient

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:21.360
and the relatively small amount of land mass it would take to create a landfill to deal with it all

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:26.640
makes it seem like the futuristic dystopia that we see in Wall-E should be impossible.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:31.000
There is no way a city could fill up with garbage like this this quickly

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:33.700
